public class Solution {
    public boolean checkValidString(String s) {
        int minOpen = 0;
        int maxOpen = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);

            if (c == '(') {
                minOpen++;
                maxOpen++;
            } else if (c == ')') {
                minOpen--;
                maxOpen--;
            } else { // c == '*'
                minOpen--; // treat '*' as ')'
                maxOpen++; // treat '*' as '('
            }

            // More closing brackets than opening brackets can handle
            if (maxOpen < 0) 
                return false;
            

            // minOpen cannot drop below zero
             if (minOpen < 0) 
                minOpen = 0;
        
        }

        return minOpen == 0;
    }
}





The core intuition behind this greedy approach for Valid Parenthesis String relies on tracking a range of possible open bracket counts 
rather than trying every combination of * (which would cause an exponential time complexity explosion).At any index, because * can act 
as (, ), or "" (empty), we don't have just a single count of open brackets—we have a range of valid open bracket counts: [minOpen, 
maxOpen].
1. What minOpen and maxOpen RepresentmaxOpen: The maximum possible number of unmatched ( we could have if we treated every * 
aggressively as an opening parenthesis (.minOpen: The minimum possible number of unmatched ( we could have if we treated every * 
aggressively as a closing parenthesis ).
2. How Each Character Updates the Range(: Every branch increases the open count by 1 
$\rightarrow$ both minOpen and maxOpen increment.): Every branch decreases the open count by 1 $\rightarrow$ both minOpen and maxOpen 
decrement.*: Since * can be (, ), or "", the possible open count range expands. The lowest bound drops by treating * as ) (minOpen--), 
and the upper bound rises by treating * as ( (maxOpen++).
3. The Two Key Invariants / Checksif (maxOpen < 0) return false;Even under 
the most optimistic scenario (treating every wildcard as (), we encountered more closing brackets ) than opening ones. The string is 
permanently invalid at this point.if (minOpen < 0) minOpen = 0;minOpen going below 0 means we treated a wildcard * as a ) when we didn't 
actually need to (there were no open brackets to match). Since * can also act as an empty string "", we reset minOpen back to 0 instead 
of letting it stay negative.
4. The Final Check: return minOpen == 0;At the end of the string, a valid parenthesis sequence must end 
with 0 unmatched open brackets. If minOpen == 0, it guarantees that there exists at least one valid combination of * choices that 
leaves us with perfectly balanced parentheses.
